  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the RAS–MAPK signaling pathway at the cell membrane and maps several developmental syndromes onto the proteins they affect. An extracellular signal activates a cellular receptor, which recruits adaptor proteins such as SHC, GRB2, SOS1, and PTPN11 to stimulate RAS-family GTPases. These include RAS, RRAS, KRAS, and HRAS, shown switching into a GTP-bound active state. Negative regulators such as neurofibromin/NF1, p120GAP, and SPRED1 dampen this signaling.\n\nDownstream, active RAS signals through RAF kinases, especially RAF1 and BRAF, then to MEK1/2 and ERK1/2, illustrating the core cascade that transmits growth signals into the cell. Several syndrome labels are placed next to specific pathway components, visually linking disease to pathway dysregulation. Noonan syndrome and NSML are connected to multiple upstream and downstream nodes, suggesting broad involvement across the pathway. Costello is associated with HRAS, Legius with SPRED1, NF1 with neurofibromin, CM-AVM with p120GAP-related regulation, and CFC with KRAS/BRAF/MEK.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that these related syndromes arise from mutations in different parts of one tightly connected growth-factor signaling network.",
